The original reads `mesg->numFields` and indexes `mesg->fields[i]` through `mesg` on every pass. This version copies the field count and the field-array pointer into locals once, then scans with a plain pointer. Results are the same: the first matching index is returned, otherwise `FIT_UINT16_INVALID`.

The speedup should be small. It lets the compiler keep both values in registers, but I haven't measured it. A larger gain would need a faster `GetMesg` lookup, which this function can't change.

```cpp
const FIT_UINT16 Profile::GetFieldIndex(const FIT_UINT16 mesgNum, const FIT_UINT8 fieldNum)
{
    const MESG *mesg = GetMesg(mesgNum);

    if (mesg == NULL)
        return FIT_UINT16_INVALID;

    const FIT_UINT16 numFields = mesg->numFields;
    const FIELD *fields = mesg->fields;

    for (FIT_UINT16 i = 0; i < numFields; i++)
    {
        if (fields[i].num == fieldNum)
            return i;
    }

    return FIT_UINT16_INVALID;
}
```